{"id": "sevt_01QG5sDSgsMrBVoaArU3ohPg", "content": [{"text": "Confirmed. The first per-row query appears at 14:31:18.496Z \u2014 the exact deploy timestamp \u2014 and a single request (`req_efcce8808bfe`) fires many `SELECT * FROM order_items WHERE order_id = ?` calls in sequence (3,320 total in the window).\n\n## What happened\n- **14:31:18** \u2014 checkout deploy `a3f9c21` (\"refactor order summary builder\") ships.\n- **14:31** \u2014 checkout p99 jumps from ~75ms to ~3,900ms in the same minute, then drifts up to ~5s by 14:59.\n- **14:40** \u2014 error rate lifts off 0 (to 0.2\u20130.6) as requests start timing out under the latency.\n\n## Evidence\n- Deploy timestamp lines up exactly with the p99 cliff (14:31 deploy, 14:31 p99 moves).\n- The diff replaced a single batched query (`OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id__in=ids)` + in-memory grouping) with a per-order query inside the loop \u2014 a textbook N+1.\n- Log confirms: one `build_summary` request now emits one `order_items` query per order, starting at the deploy instant. This is the #1 item on the \"things that have burned us\" list.\n\nThe other 14:55 inventory deploy is after the latency was already elevated and doesn't match the onset.\n\n> **Root cause:** `a3f9c21` \u2014 the order-summary refactor turned a single batched `order_id__in` lookup into a per-order query inside a loop (N+1), blowing up checkout p99 and driving timeout-induced errors.\n\nRecommended next step: roll back `a3f9c21` (or restore the batched `order_id__in` query) to recover checkout latency.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T05:49:18.459768Z", "type": "agent.message"}
